# EW ORACLE SCRIPT VARIABLES

############################################
#
#############################
# EW STANDARD CONFIGURATION
#############################
# This config file assumes that you are running the
# standardized EW hardware configuration:
# C:\  IDE HARD DRIVE with system files and swap file
# D:\  CD-ROM
# E: - J:  6 (18 GB SCSI drives)
#
#
# Performance is optimized around the insertion/retrieval
# of parametric data.
# Reading and writing of Events, Origins, Magnitudes, Picks
# and Amplitudes should be executed near-instantaneously.  < 100 ms
# Any waveform related operations are permitted longer 
# delays, since they should involve applications that must
# process waveform data, which is typically a longer ( > 30 s )
# process.
# 
# To optimize parameteric performance, we would like to do the following
# 1) Keep as much parametric and cooked infrastructure data in memory
#    as possible.
# 2) Spread simultaneous disk activity required for parametric operations
#    across as many disks as possible.
#
#
#  Assumption: typical activity for a parametric write, would include 
#  the following: (Pick entry is used for example)
#  1) Write to the Pick table.
#  2) Write to the Pick index.
#  3) Write to the Redo Log.
#  4) Write to the Rollback Segment.
#  5) Write to the Swap file.
#  6) Possible write to the system tablespace ???
#
#
#  With this in mind, we would like to do the follwing:
#
#  1) Maximize Buffer Cache and Shared Pool Size
#     By maximizing Buffer Cache, we can keep more 
#     Parametric blocks in memory, and
#     we can handle a burst of reads/writes
#     without having to access Parametric data on Disk.
#     By making shared Pool Size large enough we can
#     keep neccessary cursors and Stored Procedures in memory.
#
#     We expect the Parametric data space to include up to 300 MB of data
#     We expect the Cooked Infrastructure data space to include up to 20 MB of data.
#     We expect the Index data space to include up to 25 MB of data.
#
#     The system tablespace will also invariable take up space
#     in cache.  (somewhere betwee 15MB and 300MB)
#
#
#     The database is designed to hold an average of 8 days of data.
#     With a favorable record distribution, we might be able to 
#     assume, that records from the last 24 hours would require
#     1/3 of the blocks from the parametric tablespace.
#     So, before considering SnippetSpace requirements, we would desire
#     a buffer cache of atleast (300/3 + 20 + 25 + 15) or 160 MB.
#     Considering the inexpensiveness of memory, and the importance
#     of the applications, we would wish to push for as large a 
#     buffer cache as possible ( > 500 MB), but would expect a minmum
#     of 200 MB.
#
#     No idea what the required Shared Pool Size would be.  80MB would
#     probably be more than sufficient.
#
#
#  2) Divide simultaneous writes among as many disks as possible, and
#     assign disks as follows:
#
#  C:    OS files, Swap file, Application Logs  (State Manager)
#  E:    System Data (System TableSpace)
#  F:    Rollback Segment  (Undo TableSpace)  not-separating Parametric and Waveform
#                                 undo disk activity may become a large bottleneck.
#                                 especially during waveform deletion.
#  G:    Redo Log
#  H:    Parametric Data / Parametric Index / Cooked Infrastructure Data & Index
#                                            (In a perfect world with non-striped
#                                             disks, we would put the tables and
#                                             the indexes on different disks.
#                                             but, instead we're going to hope that
#                                             we can keep enough data in memory,
#                                             and are gross i/o loads are low enough
#                                             that we will be able to avoid any 
#                                             single disk i/o overloads, even during
#                                             spirt activity.)
#  I:    Primary Snippet data  4x4GB
#  J:    Primary Snippet data  4x4GB
#  
############################################
############################################



# Instance configuration
# EW_ORACLE_BASE is the base directory for oracle files
# EW_ORACLE_HOME is the relative ORACLE_HOME subdirectory,
#   such that executable oracle files are expected to be in
#   $(EW_ORACLE_BASE)\$(EW_ORACLE_HOME)\bin
# EW_ORACLE_SID is the SID of the Oracle instance
#   the core db instance files will be put in
#   $(EW_ORACLE_BASE)\oradata\$(EW_ORACLE_SID)\
# EW_ORACLE_DOMAIN is the domain of the SID
#   this is used in the init file as a domain listing
EW_ORACLE_BASE c:\oracle
EW_ORACLE_HOME ora92
EW_ORACLE_SID  ewdb
EW_ORACLE_DOMAIN usgs


# EW_ORACLE_CONTROL_DIRX
# These are the directories where the db instance control files
#   will be placed.  The default is 
#   $(EW_ORACLE_BASE)\oradata\$(EW_ORACLE_SID)\
EW_ORACLE_CONTROL_DIR1  E:\oradata\ewdb
EW_ORACLE_CONTROL_DIR2  F:\oradata\ewdb
EW_ORACLE_CONTROL_DIR3  G:\oradata\ewdb


# EW_ORACLE_LOG_DIRX
# These are the directories where the db instance log files
#   will be placed.  The default is 
#   $(EW_ORACLE_BASE)\oradata\$(EW_ORACLE_SID)
EW_ORACLE_LOG_DIR1 G:\oradata\ewdb
#EW_ORACLE_LOG_DIR2
#EW_ORACLE_LOG_DIR3

# SYSTEM_PSWD is the password for the db instance "system" user - 
#   default earthworm
# SYS_PSWD is the password for the db "sys" user
#   default earthworm1
#SYSTEM_PSWD earthworm
#SYS_PSWD earthworm1

# EW_SYSTEM_DIR
# The directory where the instance System tablespace datafile
#   will be placed.  The default is 
#   $(EW_ORACLE_BASE)\oradata\$(EW_ORACLE_SID)\
EW_SYSTEM_DIR E:\oradata\ewdb

# EW_ROLLBACK_TABLESPACE is the oracle tablespace to be used for
#   creating the EW rollback segments.  The default tablespace
#   under 9i is UNDOTBS1
#EW_ROLLBACK_TABLESPACE UNDOTBS1

# EW_UNDO_DIR
# The directory where the db undo tablespace datafile
#   will be placed.  The default is 
#   $(EW_ORACLE_BASE)\oradata\$(EW_ORACLE_SID)\
EW_UNDO_DIR H:\oradata\ewdb

# EW_DATA_XXX
#   These are the directories where the EW Oracle datafiles will
#   be placed.
#   EW_DATA_CORE EW_DATA_INFRA and EW_DATA_WAVEFORM_1 all default
#   to $(EW_ORACLE_BASE)\oradata\$(EW_ORACLE_SID)\
#   all other WAVEFORM data files default to the location of 
#   EW_DATA_WAVEFORM_1
EW_DATA_CORE  I:\oradata\ewdb
EW_DATA_INFRA J:\oradata\ewdb
EW_DATA_WAVEFORM_1 I:\oradata\ewdb
EW_DATA_WAVEFORM_2 J:\oradata\ewdb
#EW_DATA_WAVEFORM_3 g:\oradata\ewdb
#EW_DATA_WAVEFORM_4 h:\oradata\ewdb
#EW_DATA_WAVEFORM_5 i:\oradata\ewdb
#EW_DATA_WAVEFORM_6 j:\oradata\ewdb

